  "lessons": [
    {
      "domain_id": "principles",
      "title": "Security Principles \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "CIA triad: confidentiality (only the right eyes see it), integrity (it is not altered), availability (it is there when needed). Three words that organize nearly every security question you will ever face.",
        "Authentication factors: something you know (password), something you have (phone, token), something you are (fingerprint). Multi-factor combines different types, so one stolen secret is not enough.",
        "Cryptography basics: symmetric uses one shared key (fast, but sharing the key is the hard part); asymmetric uses a public key to lock and a private key to open; hashing fingerprints data one-way for integrity.",
        "Risk = threat x vulnerability x impact, in plain language: a bad actor, a weakness they can use, and harm if they succeed. Reduce any of the three and risk falls.",
        "Least privilege: give every person and process the minimum access the job needs \u2014 nothing more. It limits both accidents and attackers."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Password alone: attacker with a breach dump logs straight in.\nPassword + phone approval: the same stolen password goes nowhere.",
        "walkthrough": "The second factor did not make the password stronger \u2014 it made the password insufficient on its own. That is the whole idea of defense in depth: no single failure hands over the keys."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: thinking a username plus a password is two factors \u2014 it is one factor used twice.",
        "Common trap: confusing encryption with hashing \u2014 encryption is reversible with the key; hashing is one-way.",
        "Common trap: believing more access is helpful \u2014 extra permissions multiply the damage of every mistake and breach.",
        "Common trap: treating risk as only a technical problem \u2014 people and process are usually the weakest link."
      ],
      "try_it": "Verify a file's hash yourself in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "bcdr",
      "title": "Business Continuity & Incident Response \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Business continuity (BCP) keeps the business running through disruption: alternate sites, communications, and the processes that matter most. Disaster recovery (DRP) is the IT subset \u2014 restoring servers, data, and networks.",
        "Backups must survive the disaster: keep copies offline or off-site so ransomware and fires cannot reach them, and practice restores \u2014 an untested backup is a rumor.",
        "RTO is how fast a system must be back; RPO is how much data loss is tolerable. The business states them; IT builds recovery to meet them.",
        "Incident response order: detect, contain, investigate, eradicate, recover, learn. Containment before cleanup \u2014 wiping first destroys evidence and lets the threat spread.",
        "Tabletop exercises test the plan around a table for the cost of an afternoon; lessons-learned reviews convert each incident into a better plan. Blame-free, or people hide the truth."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Ransomware encrypts the file server AND the network backup.\nThe offline drive in the drawer: untouched. Recovery by lunch.",
        "walkthrough": "The disaster reached everything it could touch. Continuity planning is about deciding in advance what it cannot touch \u2014 and proving the restore works before you need it."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: backing up to the same network the ransomware can reach \u2014 that is a copy, not a backup.",
        "Common trap: wiping an infected machine before containing and investigating \u2014 speed that destroys evidence.",
        "Common trap: writing a plan and never testing it \u2014 paper plans fail at the first outdated phone number.",
        "Common trap: skipping the lessons-learned review \u2014 the incident repeats, with interest."
      ],
      "try_it": "Confirm a backup exists in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "access-control",
      "title": "Access Control Concepts \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Three classic models: DAC \u2014 the data owner decides who gets access; MAC \u2014 the system enforces access from security labels, no overrides; RBAC \u2014 permissions attach to roles, people inherit them by role.",
        "Least privilege and need-to-know: access follows job necessity, not curiosity, seniority, or convenience. Start small; add only with justification.",
        "Separation of duties: split sensitive tasks so no one person controls a process end to end \u2014 fraud then requires collusion, which is rarer and easier to catch.",
        "Account lifecycle: provision promptly on arrival, adjust on role change, and deprovision immediately on departure. Stale accounts are attacker favorites.",
        "Physical controls matter: badges, locks, visitor sign-in, and a culture where everyone challenges tailgaters. The best firewall cannot stop a held door."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "One employee issues refunds AND approves them \u2192 $9,000 in fake refunds.\nSplit the duties \u2192 the same fraud now needs two conspirators.",
        "walkthrough": "No technology was purchased. Moving one approval to a second person changed the fraud from a solo act into a conspiracy \u2014 that is the power of a process control."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: giving new hires full access to make onboarding easy \u2014 convenience now, breach later.",
        "Common trap: holding the door for a stranger with coffee \u2014 politeness is the tailgater's tool.",
        "Common trap: forgetting to disable accounts when people leave \u2014 audit the leavers, not just the joiners.",
        "Common trap: confusing identification (who you claim to be) with authentication (proving it)."
      ],
      "try_it": "Audit a shared folder's permissions in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "network-security",
      "title": "Network Security \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "A firewall enforces rules about what traffic may enter or leave \u2014 a bouncer with a guest list. It controls the door; it does not encrypt files or replace passwords.",
        "Segmentation separates trust levels: guest Wi-Fi away from payment systems, servers away from workstations. A breach in one zone should not mean a breach in all zones.",
        "A VPN encrypts traffic between a device and a trusted network, defeating local eavesdroppers on public Wi-Fi. It protects the path, not the endpoints.",
        "Wireless basics: WPA2/WPA3 with a strong passphrase, unique admin credentials, updated firmware. WEP is broken; hidden network names are obscurity, not security.",
        "DDoS attacks overwhelm availability with floods from botnets \u2014 mitigate upstream with CDNs, scrubbing, and scaling, not with a bigger firewall rule.",
        "Attack surface is the sum of reachable entry points. Disable unneeded services, close unneeded ports, and re-check: every open door is a door you must defend."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Cafe Wi-Fi + point-of-sale on one network \u2192 any customer can reach the card terminal.\nGuest network segmented away \u2192 the terminal is invisible to guests.",
        "walkthrough": "Segmentation does not make either network more secure in isolation \u2014 it stops a problem in one from becoming a problem in the other. Boundaries are the architecture."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: assuming the firewall makes everything behind it safe \u2014 one phished laptop is already inside.",
        "Common trap: leaving WEP or default passwords on wireless gear \u2014 both are public knowledge to attackers.",
        "Common trap: treating a bigger pipe as DDoS protection \u2014 floods scale faster than your bandwidth.",
        "Common trap: leaving forgotten services running \u2014 the Telnet on that old printer is an unlocked window."
      ],
      "try_it": "List listening services in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "sec-operations",
      "title": "Security Operations \u2014 Core concepts",
      "concepts": [
        "Logging is the foundation: without logs you cannot detect, investigate, or scope incidents. Centralize them (often in a SIEM) so one search covers everything.",
        "Patch management is a cycle: inventory what you have, test the fix, deploy promptly, verify. Known vulnerabilities with available patches cause most breaches.",
        "Hardening means secure by default: change default credentials, disable unneeded services, apply baselines before systems touch the network.",
        "Awareness turns people into sensors: teach the signs of phishing, make reporting one click, and celebrate reports. Punishment drives incidents underground.",
        "Data classification (Public, Internal, Confidential) drives handling rules: encryption, sharing limits, retention, and destruction matched to sensitivity.",
        "Encryption at rest protects data on lost devices; encryption in transit protects it on hostile networks. Know which one each situation needs."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "No logs \u2192 'we think we're clean, but we can't prove it.'\nCentralized logs \u2192 'the attacker entered Tuesday at 2 AM via this account.'",
        "walkthrough": "The difference between guessing and knowing is the log trail. Operations is mostly this: collect the evidence routinely, so the investigation is possible when it counts."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Common trap: treating the SIEM as a replacement for analysts \u2014 it focuses judgment; it does not replace it.",
        "Common trap: postponing patches because nothing has happened yet \u2014 quiet is not the same as safe.",
        "Common trap: assuming awareness training is a one-time video \u2014 behavior change needs repetition and measurement.",
        "Common trap: encrypting everything identically \u2014 match protection to classification, or nothing gets the protection it needs."
      ],
      "try_it": "Spot failed logins in the Terminal tab \u2192"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "principles",
      "title": "Security Principles: CIA, Risk, Controls, and Governance",
      "concepts": [
        "The CIA triad is the foundation of all security thinking. Confidentiality ensures only authorized parties can access data \u2014 achieved through encryption, access controls, and classification. Integrity ensures data is accurate and is changed only by authorized actions \u2014 achieved through hashing, checksums, digital signatures, and change control. Availability ensures data and systems are reachable when needed \u2014 achieved through redundancy, backups, patching, and denial-of-service defenses. Nearly every exam question about 'which principle' maps to one of these three.",
        "Risk is the combination of a threat (anything that could cause harm, such as ransomware) and a vulnerability (a weakness the threat can exploit, such as unpatched software). Formally, risk exists where a threat meets a vulnerability and an asset is exposed. Qualitative risk analysis ranks risks with labels like high/medium/low; quantitative analysis uses numbers: single loss expectancy (SLE) is the cost of one incident, annualized rate of occurrence (ARO) is how often it is expected per year, and annualized loss expectancy (ALE) equals SLE times ARO. Controls are judged by comparing their cost against the ALE reduction they deliver.",
        "Security controls are described two ways. By function: preventive controls stop incidents (firewalls, mantraps, least privilege), detective controls spot them (IDS, CCTV, log monitoring), corrective controls restore normal operations (backups, incident response), deterrent controls discourage attackers (warning banners, visible guards), and compensating controls substitute when the ideal control is impractical. By category: administrative controls are policies and procedures, technical (logical) controls are hardware and software mechanisms, and physical controls are tangible barriers and devices. Defense in depth layers controls across both dimensions so no single failure is fatal.",
        "Governance turns security intent into organizational reality. Policies are high-level statements of management intent; standards mandate specific technologies or configurations; procedures are step-by-step instructions; guidelines are recommended (not mandatory) best practices; baselines define minimum security configurations. Data classification (for example public, internal, confidential, restricted) drives handling rules, with data owners deciding classification, custodians implementing protections, and users following the rules.",
        "Professional ethics matter at the entry level too. The ISC2 Code of Ethics binds members to four canons: protect society, the commonwealth, and the infrastructure; act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, and legally; provide diligent and competent service to principals; and advance and protect the profession. When a scenario pits convenience against integrity, the ethical answer follows these canons."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "shell",
        "code": "# Quantitative risk: is a $12,000/year control worth it?\nSLE=40000        # cost of one breach\nARO_BEFORE=0.5   # once every two years\nARO_AFTER=0.1    # with the control\nCOST=12000\n\nALE_BEFORE=$(echo \"$SLE * $ARO_BEFORE\" | bc)\nALE_AFTER=$(echo \"$SLE * $ARO_AFTER\" | bc)\nSAVINGS=$(echo \"$ALE_BEFORE - $ALE_AFTER\" | bc)\n\necho \"ALE before: \\$$ALE_BEFORE /yr\"\necho \"ALE after:  \\$$ALE_AFTER /yr\"\necho \"Savings:    \\$$SAVINGS /yr vs cost \\$$COST /yr\"\nif [ \"$SAVINGS\" -gt \"$COST\" ]; then\n  echo \"Decision: IMPLEMENT (savings exceed cost)\"\nelse\n  echo \"Decision: REJECT (cost exceeds savings)\"\nfi",
        "walkthrough": "This script implements the ALE = SLE x ARO formula from the lesson. It computes the annualized loss expectancy before the control ($20,000) and after ($4,000), derives the $16,000 yearly savings, and compares it to the $12,000 control cost. Because savings exceed cost, the control is cost-justified \u2014 the same arithmetic behind scenario isc2-cc-sc2. Notice the script never claims risk reaches zero: a residual ALE of $4,000 remains for management to accept, transfer, or reduce further."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Encryption protects confidentiality, not integrity \u2014 an encrypted file can still be corrupted; integrity needs hashing or signatures.",
        "Risk can be reduced, transferred (insurance), accepted, or avoided \u2014 but never eliminated entirely.",
        "Deterrent is not the same as preventive: a warning banner discourages but does not block, while a firewall blocks.",
        "Policies state intent; procedures give steps. Mixing them up is a classic exam trap."
      ],
      "try_it": "Walk through your home setup and name one preventive, one detective, and one corrective control you already have (for example: a door lock, a smoke detector, and a backup drive). Then classify each as administrative, technical, or physical.",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "d1",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/isc2-cc"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "principles",
      "title": "Continuity, Recovery, and Incident Response",
      "concepts": [
        "Business continuity planning (BCP) keeps the business operating during a disruption; disaster recovery planning (DRP) restores IT systems afterward. The business impact analysis (BIA) is the foundation of both: it identifies critical processes, their dependencies, and how much pain an outage causes. From the BIA come the recovery targets: the recovery time objective (RTO) is the maximum tolerable downtime, the recovery point objective (RPO) is the maximum tolerable data loss measured in time, and the maximum tolerable downtime (MTD) is the absolute limit before the business faces existential harm. RTO must never exceed MTD, and RPO drives backup frequency.",
        "Backups are the core corrective control. A full backup copies everything; an incremental backup copies changes since the last backup of any kind (fast to take, slow to restore \u2014 you need the full plus every incremental); a differential backup copies changes since the last full (slower to take than incremental, faster to restore \u2014 you need only the full plus the latest differential). The widely cited 3-2-1 rule keeps three copies of data on two different media with one copy offsite. Backups must be tested \u2014 an untested backup is a hope, not a control \u2014 and at least one copy should be offline or immutable so ransomware cannot encrypt it.",
        "Recovery sites come in three flavors. A hot site is fully equipped and can take over in hours but costs the most. A warm site has hardware and connectivity but needs configuration, taking days. A cold site is just space and power, taking weeks but costing the least. The choice follows the RTO: the shorter the RTO, the hotter (and pricier) the site. Reciprocal agreements, where two organizations host each other's recovery capability, are a budget alternative.",
        "Incident response follows NIST Special Publication 800-61's four-phase lifecycle. Preparation builds the team, tools, playbooks, and communication plans before anything happens. Detection and Analysis identifies and triages potential incidents using alerts, logs, and user reports. Containment, Eradication, and Recovery limits the blast radius, removes the cause, and restores systems to normal. Post-Incident Activity captures lessons learned and feeds improvements back into preparation. Skipping straight to recovery without containment and eradication is how incidents recur.",
        "Communication discipline matters during incidents: know who declares an incident, who talks to executives, legal, and regulators, and what gets documented for the post-incident review. Evidence handling \u2014 preserving logs and forensic images with a documented chain of custody \u2014 keeps response options (including legal ones) open."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "INCIDENT: Ransomware notes on 3 workstations, 08:04 Monday\n\nPhase 1 - Preparation (already done before the incident):\n  IR team roster, playbook, and call tree existed; backups\n  were tested last month; an offline backup copy is available.\n\nPhase 2 - Detection and Analysis:\n  08:04  Help desk tickets + ransom notes = confirmed incident\n  08:15  Analyst scopes: 3 hosts, same subnet, same file share\n\nPhase 3 - Containment, Eradication, and Recovery:\n  08:20  CONTAIN: disconnect the 3 hosts from the network\n  09:00  ERADICATE: reimage hosts from known-good media\n  10:30  RECOVER: restore file share from offline backup,\n           verify hashes, return to service\n\nPhase 4 - Post-Incident Activity:\n  Root cause: unpatched SMB service. Actions: emergency\n  patching, network segmentation for the subnet, phishing\n  refresher, playbook update. Lessons-learned report filed.",
        "walkthrough": "This walkthrough applies the NIST 800-61 lifecycle to the ransomware scenario from isc2-cc-sc4. Note the ordering discipline: containment (isolate) comes before eradication (reimage), which comes before recovery (restore) \u2014 restoring before eradicating would just re-infect the systems. Preparation done earlier (tested, offline backups) is what makes the recovery phase possible at all. The post-incident phase converts the pain into durable improvements, closing the loop back to preparation."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Restoring from backup before eradicating the malware re-infects the systems \u2014 containment and eradication come first.",
        "Confusing RTO (how fast to recover) with RPO (how much data you can lose) \u2014 RPO drives backup frequency, RTO drives recovery-site choice.",
        "Incremental restores need the full plus EVERY incremental in sequence; forgetting one breaks the chain.",
        "An untested backup is not a control. Test restores on a schedule and document the results."
      ],
      "try_it": "Write a one-page incident playbook for 'laptop stolen from a car': list your detection step, your containment step, your eradication/recovery steps, and two lessons-learned actions. Time yourself \u2014 playbooks are written before the incident, not during it.",
      "id": "cbt-None",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "d2",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/isc2-cc"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "principles",
      "title": "Access Control: IAAA, MFA, Protocols, and Models",
      "concepts": [
        "Access control runs on IAAA: Identification (claiming an identity, e.g., typing a username), Authentication (proving it, e.g., entering a password), Authorization (what the proven identity is allowed to do), and Accountability (logging actions so they can be traced to the identity). Authentication must precede authorization \u2014 the system cannot decide what you may do until it knows who you are \u2014 and accountability depends on the logs capturing all of it.",
        "Authentication factors come in three categories: something you know (passwords, PINs), something you have (phones, hardware tokens, smart cards), and something you are (fingerprints, face, voice). Multi-factor authentication (MFA) requires two or more factors from different categories; two steps within the same category (password plus security question) is still single-factor. Biometrics are convenient but irrevocable \u2014 you cannot reissue a fingerprint \u2014 so they are best paired with another factor and protected accordingly.",
        "Common authentication protocols each have a home turf. Kerberos is the ticket-based LAN standard (used by Active Directory). LDAP (often LDAPS) provides directory lookups for identity data. RADIUS and TACACS+ centralize AAA for network devices, with TACACS+ separating authentication, authorization, and accounting and encrypting the full payload. SAML 2.0 is the XML standard for federated web single sign-on via identity-provider assertions. OAuth 2.0 is an authorization/delegation framework \u2014 often paired with OpenID Connect for authentication \u2014 dominant in APIs and mobile apps. CHAP and PAP are legacy PPP authentication protocols; EAP frameworks underpin WPA-Enterprise wireless authentication.",
        "Authorization models decide who gets what. DAC (discretionary) lets data owners grant access at their discretion, as with file ACLs. MAC (mandatory) has the system enforce access from security labels and clearances that users cannot override \u2014 the government and military model. RBAC (role-based) assigns permissions to job roles and users to roles, simplifying administration and supporting least privilege. ABAC (attribute-based) evaluates attributes of the subject, resource, action, and environment for fine-grained, context-aware decisions. Rule-based access control applies global rules (such as time-of-day restrictions) uniformly.",
        "Account management and physical controls complete the picture. Provisioning grants access on hire or role change, deprovisioning revokes it promptly on departure \u2014 orphaned accounts are a classic attack path. Least privilege gives users only the access their role requires, enforced through periodic access reviews and separation of duties for sensitive processes. Physical controls \u2014 mantraps, badge readers, biometrics, CCTV, bollards, cable locks, visitor logs \u2014 protect the facilities where the technical controls live."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "DECISION: partner engineers need web-portal access\n\nRequirement: sign in once with the corporate identity\nprovider, then use the partner's browser-based CAD portal\nwith no second login. The partner must trust our\nauthentication.\n\nCandidate protocols:\n  Kerberos .... LAN ticket system; does not cross the\n                public internet to another organization.\n  RADIUS ...... AAA for network devices, not browser SSO.\n  OAuth 2.0 ... delegation for APIs/mobile apps; the\n                classic enterprise web-SSO standard is older.\n  SAML 2.0 .... XML assertions from an identity provider\n                to a service provider; built for federated\n                browser SSO.\n\nChoice: SAML 2.0. The corporate IdP issues signed\nassertions; the partner's portal (service provider)\nvalidates them and creates the session. No partner-side\npasswords to manage, and deprovisioning at the IdP\ninstantly cuts partner access too.",
        "walkthrough": "This is the reasoning behind scenario isc2-cc-sc9. The key discriminator is 'browser-based SSO across organizational boundaries' \u2014 that phrase points to federation, and the enterprise federation standard is SAML 2.0. OAuth 2.0 plus OpenID Connect is the modern alternative many SaaS apps use, but SAML remains the canonical answer for cross-company web SSO. Note the operational bonus: centralizing authentication at the identity provider means one deprovisioning action revokes access everywhere."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Two steps are not automatically MFA \u2014 a password plus a security question is still one factor (something you know).",
        "Authentication proves who you are; authorization decides what you may do. SSO authenticates once \u2014 it does not grant universal authorization.",
        "SSO is convenient but a single point of failure: protect the identity provider with MFA and monitor it closely.",
        "Biometrics identify but cannot be revoked \u2014 never use them as the sole factor for high-value access."
      ],
      "try_it": "List every account you own that protects money or identity. Mark which have MFA enabled and which factor types they use. Enable MFA (preferably an authenticator app or hardware key over SMS) on any that lack it.",
      "id": "cbt-None",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "d3",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/isc2-cc"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "principles",
      "title": "Network Security: Layers, Segmentation, and Wireless",
      "concepts": [
        "The OSI model gives every attack an address. Layer 1 (Physical): wiretaps, signal jamming. Layer 2 (Data Link): MAC flooding, ARP spoofing/poisoning. Layer 3 (Network): IP spoofing, ICMP attacks, rogue routing. Layer 4 (Transport): SYN floods, port scanning, session hijacking at the TCP level. Layer 5 (Session): session hijacking and fixation. Layer 6 (Presentation): SSL/TLS stripping and encryption abuse. Layer 7 (Application): phishing, SQL injection, cross-site scripting (XSS), DNS poisoning, and malware delivery. Mapping an attack to its layer immediately suggests the right control.",
        "Segmentation limits blast radius. VLANs separate broadcast domains at Layer 2; subnetting separates them at Layer 3; a DMZ (demilitarized zone) is a screened subnet for public-facing services between the internet and the internal network; microsegmentation applies fine-grained policy around individual workloads. The design rule is simple: internet-facing services live in the DMZ, sensitive data lives inside, and firewalls enforce least-privilege traffic between zones.",
        "Firewalls and intrusion systems are complementary, not interchangeable. Packet-filtering firewalls judge individual packets by header fields; stateful firewalls track connection state; next-generation firewalls (NGFW) add application awareness, user identity, and threat intelligence. An IDS (intrusion detection system) watches and alerts \u2014 passive, usually placed to monitor traffic via a span port or tap. An IPS (intrusion prevention system) sits inline and can block \u2014 active, but a misconfigured IPS can break legitimate traffic. Honeypots and honeynets are decoy systems that attract attackers and generate high-fidelity alerts.",
        "VPNs extend private networks across untrusted ones. Site-to-site VPNs connect whole networks (branch to headquarters), typically with IPsec. Remote-access VPNs connect individual users, commonly with IPsec or TLS/SSL VPNs. IPsec operates at the network layer and offers tunnel mode (gateway to gateway) and transport mode (host to host), with AH providing integrity and ESP providing confidentiality plus integrity. TLS VPNs work through the browser or a lightweight client and traverse NAT and restrictive firewalls more easily.",
        "Wireless security has evolved hard lessons. WEP is broken and must never be used; WPA2 (AES-CCMP) was the long standard; WPA3 is current, adding stronger handshakes (SAE) and forward secrecy, with WPA3-Enterprise using 802.1X and certificates for mutual authentication. Threats include rogue access points, evil twins (impersonating the legitimate SSID to harvest credentials), deauthentication attacks, and wardriving. Mitigations: WPA3-Enterprise with 802.1X, a wireless intrusion prevention system (WIPS) to find rogues, and disabling auto-connect on managed devices."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "shell",
        "code": "# Reviewing a firewall ruleset for dangerous permits\n$ cat fw-rules.txt\n-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 22 -s 10.0.0.0/8 -j ACCEPT\n-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 80 -j ACCEPT\n-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 443 -j ACCEPT\n-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 23 -j ACCEPT\n-A INPUT -p tcp --dport 3389 -s 0.0.0.0/0 -j ACCEPT\n-A INPUT -j DROP\n\n# Which services are exposed, and to whom?\n$ grep ACCEPT fw-rules.txt | awk '{print $4, $6, $8}'\n--dport 22 -s 10.0.0.0/8     # SSH: internal only. OK.\n--dport 80                  # HTTP: open to the world. Expected for a web server.\n--dport 443                 # HTTPS: open to the world. Expected.\n--dport 23                  # TELNET: open to the world. FINDING!\n--dport 3389 -s 0.0.0.0/0   # RDP: open to the world. FINDING!\n\n# Findings: Telnet (23) sends credentials in cleartext and\n# should be disabled; RDP (3389) exposed to 0.0.0.0/0 invites\n# brute force. Remediation: remove the Telnet rule, restrict\n# RDP to the management subnet or require VPN + MFA.",
        "walkthrough": "This example shows the core network-security review skill: read the ruleset, map each permitted port to a service and a source, and flag violations of least privilege. Telnet is the louder finding because it transmits credentials in cleartext (a confidentiality failure at Layer 7 riding on Layer 4). RDP open to the world is the classic brute-force target seen in scenario isc2-cc-sc15's SIEM pattern. The final default-DROP rule is the correct posture: deny everything not explicitly permitted."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "An IDS detects and alerts; only an IPS can block. Placing an IDS inline expecting prevention is a design error.",
        "NAT hides internal addresses but is not a security boundary \u2014 it was designed for address conservation.",
        "Hiding the SSID or filtering MAC addresses is obscurity, not security; both are trivially bypassed.",
        "WEP is cryptographically broken. Any question offering WEP as acceptable is a trap."
      ],
      "try_it": "On your home router's admin page, check three things: is remote administration disabled on the WAN interface, is WPS turned off, and is the Wi-Fi using WPA2 or WPA3 (not WEP, not open)? Change anything that fails.",
      "id": "cbt-None",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "d4",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/isc2-cc"
    },
    {
      "domain_id": "principles",
      "title": "Security Operations: Logging, Crypto, Malware, and Hardening",
      "concepts": [
        "Logging and monitoring turn noise into signal. Log authentication events, privilege use, system changes, and network connections; synchronize all clocks with NTP so timestamps correlate across systems; protect logs from tampering by forwarding them to a central, append-only store with restricted access; and retain them per policy and legal requirements. A SIEM (security and information event management) aggregates logs, normalizes them, correlates events into timelines, and raises alerts on defined rules \u2014 the '4,000 failures then one success' pattern from scenario isc2-cc-sc15 is exactly what correlation rules catch. SOAR platforms extend this by orchestrating automated response playbooks.",
        "Cryptography provides confidentiality, integrity, authentication, and non-repudiation. Symmetric encryption (one shared key, e.g., AES-256) is fast and suits bulk data. Asymmetric encryption (public/private key pairs, e.g., RSA, elliptic-curve ECC) solves key distribution and enables signatures but is slower. Hashing (e.g., SHA-256) is one-way: it produces a fixed-size digest for integrity checking but cannot be reversed \u2014 hashing is not encryption. HMAC combines a hash with a secret key for authenticated integrity. Digital signatures (hash encrypted with the sender's private key) prove authenticity, integrity, and non-repudiation. PKI (public key infrastructure) with certificate authorities (CAs) binds public keys to identities, underpinning TLS. Key management \u2014 generation, distribution, rotation, and destruction \u2014 is where real-world crypto usually fails.",
        "Malware comes in recognizable families. A virus needs a host file and user action; a worm self-replicates across networks without user action; a trojan masquerades as legitimate software; ransomware encrypts data and demands payment; spyware and keyloggers steal information silently; a rootkit buries itself to maintain privileged hidden access; a RAT (remote access trojan) gives attackers interactive control; logic bombs trigger on a condition. Defenses layer antivirus/EDR, patching, application allow-listing, email filtering, backups, and user awareness.",
        "Hardening reduces attack surface before attacks arrive. Apply patches promptly, disable unnecessary services and ports, remove default credentials, enforce least privilege, configure baselines and audit against them, enable host firewalls with default-deny, encrypt data at rest and in transit, and deploy endpoint protection. Every open port and running service is a promise to defend \u2014 make fewer promises.",
        "Social engineering bypasses technology by targeting people. Phishing is bulk fraudulent email; spear phishing targets specific individuals with researched lures; whaling targets executives; vishing uses voice calls; pretexting invents a scenario to extract information; baiting dangles something tempting (like an infected USB drive). Defenses: verify through independent channels, hover before clicking, report suspicious messages, and train continuously \u2014 the human is both the target and a sensor."
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "shell",
        "code": "# Integrity checking with SHA-256 hashes\n$ printf 'Contract v3 - payment $50,000\\n' > contract.txt\n$ openssl dgst -sha256 contract.txt\nSHA2-256(contract.txt)= 8f3a...c41d\n\n# The recipient recomputes the hash on arrival:\n$ openssl dgst -sha256 contract.txt\nSHA2-256(contract.txt)= 8f3a...c41d   # match -> unaltered\n\n# Now simulate tampering in transit:\n$ printf 'Contract v3 - payment $90,000\\n' > contract.txt\n$ openssl dgst -sha256 contract.txt\nSHA2-256(contract.txt)= 1b77...e902   # DIFFERENT -> tampered!\n\n# A hash detects change but not the author. For proof of\n# origin, the sender signs the hash with their private key:\n$ openssl dgst -sha256 -sign legal.key -out contract.sig contract.txt\n$ openssl dgst -sha256 -verify legal.pub -signature contract.sig contract.txt\nVerified OK",
        "walkthrough": "This demonstrates the two halves of scenario isc2-cc-sc14. Hashing alone gives integrity: any alteration, even one character, produces a completely different digest (the avalanche effect), so the recipient detects tampering by recomputing. But a hash cannot prove who created the file \u2014 an attacker could alter the file and recompute the hash. The digital signature fixes that: only Legal's private key could have produced a signature that verifies with Legal's public key, delivering authenticity and non-repudiation on top of integrity."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Hashing is not encryption \u2014 a hash cannot be 'decrypted' back into the data, and it provides no confidentiality.",
        "Encrypting with your own private key does not give confidentiality \u2014 anyone with your public key can read it; that operation is a signature.",
        "Logs without synchronized time (NTP) cannot be correlated \u2014 timestamps become fiction across systems.",
        "Security through obscurity (hidden SSIDs, secret algorithms) is not a control; Kerckhoffs's principle says the system must stay secure even when the design is public."
      ],
      "try_it": "Pick any file on your computer and run `openssl dgst -sha256` (or `certutil -hashfile` on Windows / `shasum -a 256` on macOS) on it twice. Confirm the digests match, then change one byte and watch the digest change completely.",
      "id": "cbt-None",
      "domain_id_remapped_from": "d5",
      "merged_from": "cbt-individual/isc2-cc"
    },
    {
      "title": "From Static Diagrams to Living Systems",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "A textbook diagram shows you one part in isolation \u2014 a living systems view shows you how all parts of Isc2 Cc move together in sequence",
        "The four-step systems anatomy method: (1) See the whole in context, (2) Cut it open to reveal layers, (3) Walk through the process step-by-step, (4) Pull apart into labeled pieces",
        "Apply this to any system in this course: start with the trigger action, trace through every component it touches, watch the data flow",
        "Interview/exam power: when asked 'how does X work?', narrate the journey \u2014 'it starts here, then...'. This demonstrates senior-level systems thinking"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "TRIGGER event occurs\n  \u2192 First system receives it (see the handoff)\n  \u2192 Processing happens in sequence (watch each step)\n  \u2192 State changes propagate (follow the ripple)\n  \u2192 Final outcome delivered\n\nEach arrow is a living handoff, not a static box.",
        "walkthrough": "This is the 'jet engine cutaway' for your field: instead of memorizing isolated facts, you see the entire flow as a moving sequence. Practice narrating this journey out loud."
      },
      "pitfalls": [
        "Memorizing terms without understanding the processes they participate in",
        "Drawing architecture diagrams as boxes without showing the data flow between them",
        "Answering 'how does X work?' with a definition instead of walking through the sequence"
      ],
      "try_it": "Pick any process in this course. Write out the full journey from trigger to outcome, naming every system touched."
    },
    {
      "title": "Anatomy of Security Principles",
      "domain_id": "systems-anatomy",
      "concepts": [
        "Security Principles is a multi-step process within Security Principles: trace the full sequence from initiation to completion",
        "See it as a journey: identify the trigger, follow each handoff, watch state change at every step",
        "Every step depends on the previous \u2014 a failure at any point breaks the chain. Map these dependencies",
        "Connect Security Principles to the broader Security Principles ecosystem \u2014 nothing operates in isolation"
      ],
      "example": {
        "lang": "text",
        "code": "Step 1: Initiation\n  Security Principles begins \u2014 what triggers it?\n\nStep 2: Processing\n  Data flows through \u2014 what transforms it?\n\nStep 3: Handoff\n  Result passes to next system \u2014 what is the interface?\n\nStep 4: Completion\n  Final state reached \u2014 how do you verify?",
        "walkthrough": "Apply the four-step anatomy method to Security Principles. Draw it as a flowing sequence, not a static diagram."
